    {
      "id": "jkf72vb",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Importance of Agriculture in India"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Agriculture contributes around 16-17% to India's GDP and engages 54.6% of the workforce according to the 2011 Census.",
        "India is the world's largest producer and consumer of pulses, which are leguminous crops that help restore soil nitrogen (except Arhar).",
        "Wells and tube wells constitute the largest share of irrigation in India, accounting for approximately 62%, with Uttar Pradesh having the largest irrigated area.",
        "Norman Borlaug is recognized as the Father of Green Revolution in the World, while M.S. Swaminathan is recognized as the Father of Green Revolution in India.",
        "NABARD was established on July 12, 1982 based on the recommendations of the B. Sivaraman Committee.",
        "Sikkim became India's first 100% organic state in 2016."
      ],
      "id": "et5kfrj"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "rl9pzu1",
      "content": "• Agriculture provides employment to a large part of India's population."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ktqs3cm",
      "content": "• It contributes significantly to the national income."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xhh40xu",
      "content": "• It supplies raw materials to industries such as cotton, sugar and jute industries."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4fh4l82",
      "content": "• It ensures food security for the country."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fzl4g9c",
      "content": "• It plays an important role in India's export earnings."
    },
    {
