      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Types of Farming in India"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2fu0yov",
      "content": "• Subsistence Farming – Farmers grow crops mainly for their own consumption."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7a1typ1",
      "content": "• Commercial Farming – Crops are grown for sale in markets."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qz8cstb",
      "content": "• Plantation Farming – Large-scale farming of crops like tea, coffee and rubber."
    },
    {
      "id": "m8r7p9p",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Green Revolution"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "16xt74h",
      "content": "• The Green Revolution began in India in the 1960s."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "o43qkvg",
      "content": "• It introduced high-yielding variety (HYV) seeds."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dftlm3r",
      "content": "• It increased agricultural production significantly."
    },
